arXiv:2510.26037v1 [cs.CR] 30 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-3.0pt;"><img src="2510.26037v1/figures/siraj_logo.png" id="g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:15/17;" width="15" height="17" alt="[Uncaptioned image]"></span> SIRAJ: Diverse and Efficient Red-Teaming for LLM Agents 
<br class="ltx_break">via Distilled Structured Reasoning
</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Kaiwen Zhou<sup id="id2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">♡</span></sup> Ahmed Elgohary<sup id="id3" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">♣</span></sup> A S M Iftekhar<sup id="id4" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">♣</span></sup> Amin Saied<sup id="id5" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">♣</span></sup>
</span><span id="id6" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Work done during Kaiwen Zhou’s internship at Microsoft.</span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><sup id="id7" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">♣</span></sup>Microsoft Responsible AI Research  <sup id="id8" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">♡</span></sup>University of California, Santa Cruz
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">kzhou35@ucsc.edu</span>  <span id="id10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ahmedghoneim@microsoft.com</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The ability of LLM agents to plan and invoke tools exposes them to new safety risks, making a comprehensive red-teaming system crucial for discovering vulnerabilities and ensuring their safe deployment.
We present SIRAJ: a generic red-teaming framework for arbitrary <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">black-box</span> LLM agents.
We employ a dynamic two-step process that starts with an agent definition and generates diverse seed test cases that cover various risk outcomes, tool-use trajectories, and risk sources. Then, it iteratively constructs and refines
model-based adversarial attacks based on the execution trajectories of former attempts.
To optimize the red-teaming cost, we present a model distillation approach that leverages structured forms of
a teacher model’s reasoning to train smaller models that are equally effective.
Across diverse evaluation agent settings, our seed test case generation approach yields 2 – 2.5x boost to the coverage of risk outcomes and tool-calling trajectories.
Our distilled 8B red-teamer model improves attack success rate by 100%, surpassing the 671B Deepseek-R1 model.
Our ablations and analyses validate the effectiveness of the iterative framework, structured reasoning, and the generalization of our red-teamer models.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The growing deployment of Large Language Model (LLM) agents, which leverage planning and tool use to accomplish complex tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qin et al. (2023)</a></cite>, has introduced significant safety concerns. Being able to perform unethical actions in the real world, such agents can cause more severe and diverse harm than traditional LLMs  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Debenedetti et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhan et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Andriushchenko et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vijayvargiya et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
Therefore, a critical step before deploying LLM agents in real-world applications is to conduct
comprehensive safety evaluation and red-teaming for them to uncover any potential risks they might introduce.
However, existing agent safety evaluations lack the necessary diversity, covering only coarse-grained safety categories without considering fine-grained risks for a given agent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Andriushchenko et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vijayvargiya et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
Furthermore, existing agents red-teaming frameworks are limited, either require impractical white-box access to the agent or target a narrow class of risks  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025)</a></cite>, which is insufficient for the diverse and complex threats that agents may encounter.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address these gaps, we propose SIRAJ:<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          SIRAJ: <span id="footnote1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>tructured <span id="footnote1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I</span>terative <span id="footnote1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R</span>easoning for <span id="footnote1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>gents safety <span id="footnote1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">J</span>udgement. In Arabic, Siraj means lamp/light (shining light on the safety risks of black-box agents).</span></span></span> a unified framework for agents red-teaming that only requires a black-box access to the agents under evaluation. First, to ensure diverse coverage, we focus on fine-grained risk outcomes within broader safety categories. For example, for the risk category "leak private / sensitive information", we consider various types of private information, such as user credentials, personal data, etc.
Moreover, as each fine-grained risk can be triggered in
multiple ways, we consider different ways that an agent can lead to each risk, which we represent by the action sequence (trajectory) that the agent takes as well as the risk source (a malicious user vs. a malicious environment) that lead to each risk e.g., an agent can leak a password when the user directly asks for it, or when the agent is compromised by a malicious website.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Secondly, we propose a model-based red-teaming framework that iteratively generates adversarial test cases with red-teaming strategies, based on the agent’s execution trajectories of previous attempts.
However, applying such methods with large models is costly and time-consuming. Therefore, we introduce <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the structured reasoning distillation</span> approach to train a significantly smaller red-teamer models.
We first propose a structured reasoning format for red-teaming that decomposes the reasoning process into distinct components.
Then, we collect successful red-teaming attempts from a large teacher model that is prompted to follow our structured reasoning format, which are then used to train a smaller student model via supervised fine-tuning (SFT) followed by reinforcement learning (RL) to further improve its red-teaming effectiveness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments show that the proposed seed test case generation significantly improves the evaluation diversity over baselines, achieving around 2x greater tool-calling trajectory diversity and 2.5x risk outcome diversity. Furthermore, our red-teamer distillation improves the attack success rate of Qwen3-8B by over 100%, surpasing that of Deepseek-R1 (671B). Ablation studies confirm the effectiveness of our iterative framework, structured reasoning, and both the SFT and RL training stages, showing that our red-teamer generalizes to novel agent settings and risk types.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions include: 1) A two-step seed test case generation method that dynamically generates safety test cases for arbitrary agents with high diversity in risk outcomes, tool-calling trajectories, and risk sources;
2) A fully black-box adversarial red-teaming method that can target diverse risks of LLM agents. Our method employs iterative adversarial refinement with the execution trajectories as feedback;
3) A novel method for reasoning model distillation in which we replace long and repetitive raw reasoning traces with concise structured forms before the distillation process;
4) Extensive experiments and ablations that demonstrate the effectiveness of each component of our framework in generating diverse test cases and adversarially attacking various LLM agents.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLM Agents Safety Evaluation and Automatic Red-teaming</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the increasing deployment of LLM agents in real-world applications, ensuring their safety and reliability has become a critical concern. Recent works have explored various aspects of LLM agents safety evaluation, including safety benchmarks on user-harmful instructions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Andriushchenko et al. (2024)</a></cite>, environment-based harms such as prompt injection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhan et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Debenedetti et al. (2024)</a></cite>, web pop-ups <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024a)</a></cite>, and the combinations of them <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vijayvargiya et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tur et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma (2025)</a></cite>.
Moreover, recent works have also investigated adversarial red-teaming to further uncover potential vulnerabilities in LLM agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
AdvAgent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2024)</a></cite> proposes to use supervised fine-tuning and DPO to train a red-teaming LLM that can generate adversarial prompt injections, but it shows weak generalization to different LLMs. AgentVigil <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025)</a></cite> also proposes a multi-round mutation strategy to generate adversarial prompt injections. UDora <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025)</a></cite> proposes an optimization-based string insertion approach to attack LLM agents, requiring a white-box access to the underlying model.
On the contrary, our approach only requires a black-box access to the evaluated agents which makes it widely applicable. Furthermore, we explicitly optimize for the diversity of the evaluation without making any assumptions about the target agents.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLM Red-teaming</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Red-teaming LLMs has been an active research topic. Jailbreak attacks have been widely studied to bypass the safety guardrails of LLMs, including
string-based attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al. (2023)</a></cite> and strategy-based attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
Among the strategy-based attacks, recent works have explored fine-tuning a red-teamer LLM to generate more effective adversarial strategies and prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ge et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beutel et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. (2025b)</a></cite>.
Besides improving attack success, recent works have also discussed the importance of diversity in red-teaming <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Samvelyan et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beutel et al. (2024)</a></cite>. This includes the coverage of different attack styles/strategies and the coverage of different safety categories.
Our work is the first to discuss and study the problem of comprehensive red-teaming for LLM agents. We achieve diversity on several angles with a two-step seed test case generation process. Our iterative adversarial transformation approach retains effective red-teaming results (i.e., high attack success rate) while being cost-efficient.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.26037v1/siraj_overview.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:438/95;" width="438" height="95" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Overview of SIRAJ: the proposed two-step red teaming framework for LLM agents.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Definitions</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM agent</span> <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is an arbitrary backbone LLM paired with a set of tools <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> that it can call (e.g., send emails, web search). To execute an <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">input task</span> (e.g., “Send me an email with last week’s Champions League updates”), <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> follows a sequence of tool calls, which we refer to as a <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">trajectory</span> <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our safety evaluation starts with a target <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">risk category</span>, e.g., “Leak private information”, which spans diverse potential fine-grained <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">risk outcomes</span> (e.g., “Leak a password”), each of which can be triggered by the task instruction (e.g., an adversarial user) or a malicious environment. We refer to the trigger as the <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">risk source</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Diverse and Efficient Automatic Red-Teaming</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the following task: Given an LLM agent <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and a set of target risk categories <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, test and uncover
as many potential fine-grained risk outcomes within each category that <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is susceptible to as possible.
A risk outcome can be triggered in various ways (agent trajectories), e.g., leaking bank account information can happen when the agent is asked to send an email, or make a post on social media. Likewise, a risk can be triggered by the user task or the environment. Hence, we expand the diversity goal of the red-teaming to further include both execution trajectories and risk source. We assume only a <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">black-box access</span> to <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and its backbone model, i.e., only execution trajectories and model outputs are accessible during the automatic red-teaming process. The effectiveness of the red-teaming process is measured in terms of the attack success rate (ASR) of the test cases, which is the ratio of test cases that yield a risky outcome. A key aspect that we consider is the efficiency of the red-teaming process. We aim to retain the effectiveness of the red-teaming process with significantly reduced compute.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Framework overview</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We explicitly disentangle the diversity and the adversarial nature of the evaluation in a two-step framework (Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ SIRAJ: Diverse and Efficient Red-Teaming for LLM Agents via Distilled Structured Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) that synthesizes risky tasks in the first step and transforms them into adversarial variants in the second step.
Given target risk categories <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and an agent definition <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, we generate seed test cases <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> while ensuring coverage of fine-grained risk outcomes, risk sources, and execution trajectories (Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Seed Test Case Generation ‣ SIRAJ: Diverse and Efficient Red-Teaming for LLM Agents via Distilled Structured Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
Then, the second step iteratively attempts to generate adversarial test cases <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> that successfully yield the pre-determined fine-grained risk outcomes, source and trajectory in the first step (Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Model-based Adversarial Red-Teaming ‣ SIRAJ: Diverse and Efficient Red-Teaming for LLM Agents via Distilled Structured Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Seed Test Case Generation</h2>

<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S4.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:134.1pt;vertical-align:-64.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-19.6pt,7.6pt) scale(0.897938752655799,0.897938752655799) ;">
<table id="S4.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
