</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Results for Seed Test Case Generation</h3>

<section id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Improved diversity.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Red-teaming evaluation metrics ‣ 6.1 Settings ‣ 6 Experiment ‣ SIRAJ: Diverse and Efficient Red-Teaming for LLM Agents via Distilled Structured Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>
shows the average number of unique tool calling trajectories within each agent configuration and safety category, and the percentage of test cases with environment adversaries.
Conditioning on formerly generated test cases significantly improves the diversity of tool calling trajectories, from 18.3 to 28.4. The outcome generation step also helps improve the trajectory diversity, from 15.6 to 18.3.
Finally, our dynamic test case generation approach significantly outperforms the static benchmark Agent-SafetyBench, not just because it enables better scaling by generating more test cases for each agent, but also because it optimizes for trajectory diversity: the number of unique trajectories is <math id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.6\pm 0.04" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.6</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.04</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.6\pm 0.04</annotation></semantics></math> when randomly downsampled to the same number of total test cases (429) as Agent-SafetyBench.
In Appendix <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Scaling Effect of Outcome Generation ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ SIRAJ: Diverse and Efficient Red-Teaming for LLM Agents via Distilled Structured Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>, we also show that the number of unique risk outcomes scales well with our method.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.26037v1/group_asr_comparison.svg" id="S6.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/154;" width="476" height="154"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Attack success rate of test agents groups under different train-test overlap in agents &amp; tools.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3 </span>Red-Teaming Main Results and Ablations</h3>

<section id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Effectiveness of iterative-red teaming.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Red-teaming evaluation metrics ‣ 6.1 Settings ‣ 6 Experiment ‣ SIRAJ: Diverse and Efficient Red-Teaming for LLM Agents via Distilled Structured Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows that history information, either the formerly generated test cases or their execution trajectories, can significantly improve the ASR, with the latter leading to larger gains.
We provide qualitative examples in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS8" title="A.8 Red-teaming Case Studies ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ SIRAJ: Diverse and Efficient Red-Teaming for LLM Agents via Distilled Structured Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training-free structured reasoning.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prompting red-teamer models to reason with our structure
improves the ASRs (5.5% average gain with R1) of different models (Table <a href="#S6.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Red-teaming evaluation metrics ‣ 6.1 Settings ‣ 6 Experiment ‣ SIRAJ: Diverse and Efficient Red-Teaming for LLM Agents via Distilled Structured Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), which suggests that a more organized and clearer reasoning process indeed helps the models to perform more effective red-teaming.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Effectiveness of structured reasoning on distillation.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Comparing different variants of the structured reasoning process in the SFT data, structured-R1’s summaries yield the largest gain, outperforming the full structured reasoning by 2% and GPT-5 structured summaries by 7.4% on average.
This is because the full reasoning of the structured R1
did not followed the reasoning structure in over 40% of the time, and is still much verbose than its just using its structured summary (Table 6).
For the GPT-5 restructured version, although it follows the structure well, its strategies and patterns are still from the unstructured R1, which are less effective than those of the structured variant. We select the structured R1 summaries, the most effective variant, as the one for RL training to produce the final SIRAJ  model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We then compare the ASRs of red-teamers trained with and without structured reasoning (Table <a href="#S6.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Red-teaming evaluation metrics ‣ 6.1 Settings ‣ 6 Experiment ‣ SIRAJ: Diverse and Efficient Red-Teaming for LLM Agents via Distilled Structured Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).
We notice that all structured reasoning variants outperform the unstructured ones, except for the one that only keeps the final answer test case, confirming the importance of the structured reasoning process.
The best SIRAJ  models improves ASR by 10.2% and 11.0% for SFT and SFT+RL. Retaining the reasoning structure during the RL phase using the format reward <math id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{r\_format}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{r\_format}</annotation></semantics></math> introduces a 1.2% average gain.
Moreover, with structured reasoning, the distilled SIRAJ  red-teamer outperforms the teacher model by 0.5% ASR, while without structured reasoning, the distilled red-teamer lags behind the teacher R1 model by 5.0%. This indicates that structured reasoning can help the distillation process to better transfer the knowledge from the teacher model to the student model. In contrast, without structured reasoning, the student model may struggle to learn effectively from the long, verbose, and noisy reasoning process.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Distillation results.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Red-teaming evaluation metrics ‣ 6.1 Settings ‣ 6 Experiment ‣ SIRAJ: Diverse and Efficient Red-Teaming for LLM Agents via Distilled Structured Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows that our trained SIRAJ  red-teamer outperforms training-free red-teamers, with a 25.8% ASR gain over the Qwen-8B baseline and a 6.0% over the R1 baseline.
RL training further improves the ASR compared to SFT only, with a 3.1% average improvement on ASR.
Moreover, compared with training-free structured variants, our red-teamer still has a 20.6% ASR advantage over Qwen3-8B and a 0.5% advantage over R1. These results confirm the effectiveness of our distillation approach, achieving comparable or even better performance than much larger models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Generalization to different backbone LLMs.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Red-teaming evaluation metrics ‣ 6.1 Settings ‣ 6 Experiment ‣ SIRAJ: Diverse and Efficient Red-Teaming for LLM Agents via Distilled Structured Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> also suggests that our trained red-teamer generalizes well to agents with different backbone LLMs and prompt settings.
Importantly, despite only being trained on GPT-5-mini-based agent, our trained model generalizes well on safer LLMs such as Safe o4-mini and Safe GPT-5-mini, which is valuable for practical applications where the safety and capabilities of LLMs are evolving over time.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.4 </span>Red-Teaming Analysis</h3>

<section id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Generalization to novel agents.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Figure <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Improved diversity. ‣ 6.2 Results for Seed Test Case Generation ‣ 6 Experiment ‣ SIRAJ: Diverse and Efficient Red-Teaming for LLM Agents via Distilled Structured Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we compare the red-teaming quality on different agent groups. Our trained red-teamer consistently improves the ASR on all agent groups, including the agents with partially or completely unseen tools by over than 20%.
We also observe that only on agents with completely unseen tools, our trained red-teamer has a lower ASR than the R1 model (in spite of the 18.7% ASR improvements with the training). We leave further boosting the generalization
to unseen tools for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Efficiency of the distilled model</h4>

<div id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To confirm the efficiency of the distilled red-teamer, we compare (Table <a href="#S6.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Efficiency of the distilled model ‣ 6.4 Red-Teaming Analysis ‣ 6 Experiment ‣ SIRAJ: Diverse and Efficient Red-Teaming for LLM Agents via Distilled Structured Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) the average number of tokens used in each seed test case either until attack success or until reaching the maximum number of iterations <math id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=3</annotation></semantics></math>. Our trained red-teamer uses significantly fewer output tokens than both R1 and structured-R1 while still achieving most of the efficiency gains by the reduced model size (8B vs 671B).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T6" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S6.T6.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:169.1pt;height:34.3pt;vertical-align:-15.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-67.3pt,13.7pt) scale(0.556727515155255,0.556727515155255) ;">
