<figure id="S6.T7" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S6.T7.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:169.1pt;height:31.9pt;vertical-align:-14.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-46.9pt,8.8pt) scale(0.643259718829107,0.643259718829107) ;">
<table id="S6.T7.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S6.T7.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T7.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S6.T7.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></td>
<td id="S6.T7.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="2"><span id="S6.T7.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">User Harm</span></td>
<td id="S6.T7.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="2"><span id="S6.T7.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Env Harm</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T7.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T7.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S6.T7.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ASR</span></td>
<td id="S6.T7.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S6.T7.2.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ASR-T</span></td>
<td id="S6.T7.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S6.T7.2.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ASR</span></td>
<td id="S6.T7.2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S6.T7.2.1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ASR-T</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T7.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T7.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Structured-R1</td>
<td id="S6.T7.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">59.9</td>
<td id="S6.T7.2.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">78.2</td>
<td id="S6.T7.2.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">46.5</td>
<td id="S6.T7.2.1.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">70.5</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T7.2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T7.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Qwen3-8B SIRAJ</td>
<td id="S6.T7.2.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">57.6</td>
<td id="S6.T7.2.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">77.4</td>
<td id="S6.T7.2.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">48.9</td>
<td id="S6.T7.2.1.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">71.6</td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span>ASR break-down between user harm and environment harm test cases.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Red-teaming performance on user harm and environment harm test cases.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further analyze the red-teaming performance on test cases where the risky action is sourced from user instructions versus the environments. Table <a href="#S6.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Efficiency of the distilled model ‣ 6.4 Red-Teaming Analysis ‣ 6 Experiment ‣ SIRAJ: Diverse and Efficient Red-Teaming for LLM Agents via Distilled Structured Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows our trained red-teamer can successfully and consistently attack both types of test cases.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion and Discussion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present a comprehensive framework for red-teaming black-box LLM agents that starts with automatically generating diverse test cases and iteratively refines adversarial variants of them via reasoning models. We further reduce the red-teaming cost significantly by distilling large reasoning models into smaller ones while retaining and even improving effectiveness. We introduce the idea of structured reasoning and show that it significantly boosts the red-teaming as well as the distillation quality. Besides establishing an end-to-end framework for a realistic safety evaluation for LLM agents,
we hope that our structured reasoning approach inspires broader research on reasoning models and effective distillation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitation</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While our framework demonstrates strong performance in red-teaming LLM agents, there are several limitations to consider. First, our training and evaluation agents do not fully simulate the complex environments and data that real-world agents might operate in, such as large-scale codebases, websites, or file storages. Incorporating complex environments and data as the inputs of the red-teaming process can be a future extension.
Second, our current framework focuses on single-agent red-teaming, and does not address unique safety risks in multi-agent systems.
Lastly, although our proposed structured-reasoning shows improvements, it may not be the optimal reasoning structure for the task. Automatic discovery of the optimal reasoning structure can be another future direction.</p>
</div>
</section>
