<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Appendix</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Experiment Details</h3>

<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training details</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our SFT training, we use a batch size of 128, a learning rate of 4e-5, and train for 5 epochs.
In the RL training, we use a global batch size of 288, a learning rate of 1.5e-6, and set the coefficient for the KL penalty to 0.03. We iterate the RL training for 1 epoch and set the group size to 6. For the coefficient for the rewards, we set <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1}=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1}=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{2}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{2}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{3}=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{3}=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{4}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{4}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Agent configuration generation</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following prior works, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhan et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Debenedetti et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Andriushchenko et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024b)</a></cite>, we define agents’ tools and toolkits as a set of python functions and classes that the agent can call.
To generate agent configurations, we first select and refine <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> toolkits from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024b)</a></cite>, containing <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> different tools, including domains such as email, slack, and travel. Then, we prompt GPT-5 to generate <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> practical combinations of toolkits as agent configurations, such as "social media + travel". For training agent generation, we use <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{1}=26" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>26</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{1}=26</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{2}=304" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>304</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{2}=304</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{3}=200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{3}=200</annotation></semantics></math>. For testing agent generation, <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{1}=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{1}=12</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{2}=123" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>123</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{2}=123</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{3}=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{3}=16</annotation></semantics></math>.
The evaluation toolkits include: Bank, Travel, Email, Web, OS, CloudIonizer, Calendar, ChatApp, Health, FileManager, Webshop, SocialMedia.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Safety categories</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adapt 7 safety categories designed for agent safety from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024b)</a></cite>, including <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Leak sensitive data / information</span>, <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Lead to property loss</span>, <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Spread unsafe information / misinformation</span>, <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Lead to physical harm</span>, <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Violate law / ethics</span>, <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Contribute to harmful / vulnerable code</span>, and <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Compromise availability</span>.
For each agent config, we first exclude safety categories that are not applicable to the agent’s toolkits (judged by GPT-5), then select 4 out of the rest safety categories to ensure each agent is tested on the same number of safety categories.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLM settings</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate adversarial red teaming on 3 different LLMs for agents, including <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">gpt-5-mini</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI (2025a)</a></cite>, <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">gpt-5</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI (2025a)</a></cite>, <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">o4-mini</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI (2025b)</a></cite>.
For these models, we adjust their reasoning effort to the lowest for cost efficiency.
Meanwhile, we test gpt-5-mini and o4-mini with an additional <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">safe</span> prompt setting, which prompts the LLM to prioritize safety and avoid risky actions. The <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">safe</span> and <span id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">default</span> prompts used are in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS10" title="A.10 Prompts Used ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ SIRAJ: Diverse and Efficient Red-Teaming for LLM Agents via Distilled Structured Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.10</span></a>.
For seed test case generation, we use GPT-5 as the test case generator and prompt it with manually curated few-shot examples of risk outcomes and test cases.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">SFT data stats</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Among the 1,950 SFT examples, 333 of them are based on 3 failure attempts, 531 of them are based on 2 failure attempts, and the remaining 1,086 examples are based on 1 failure attempt of the seed test case.
The structured reasoning SFT responses are of length 1,478.5 tokens on average (using the Qwen3 tokenizer). In contrast, the raw reasoning SFT responses are 4,975.7 tokens on average. The comparison of their length distributions is shown in Figure <a href="#A1.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ SFT data stats ‣ A.1 Experiment Details ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ SIRAJ: Diverse and Efficient Red-Teaming for LLM Agents via Distilled Structured Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.26037v1/length_hist.svg" id="A1.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/149;" width="238" height="149"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>The length distribution of structured reasoning and raw reasoning SFT data.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Effectiveness of LLM Judge for Attack Success</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We perform a human evaluation on 100 test cases randomly sampled from the evaluation trajectories of GPT-5-mini agents on the test set, where we label the safety of the agent’s execution trajectories.
We compare the human labels with the LLM judge’s labels and find that they agree on 94% of the cases, indicating that the LLM judge is effective in evaluating the safety of the agent’s execution trajectories.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T8" class="ltx_table">
<div id="A1.T8.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:293.3pt;height:22.7pt;vertical-align:-9.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-94.1pt,7.3pt) scale(0.609084657023693,0.609084657023693) ;">
